{
  "abstract": "Let $H(x)$ be the first shortcut time at which the orbit from a positive integer $x$ meets the complete orbit of some smaller positive start. For each fixed $t$, the natural density $d_t$ of $H(x)>t$ equals $\\int G\\,\\mathbf{1}_{\\{L=t\\}}\\,d\\pi$, where $G$ is the maximum inverse gain at a ternary Haar root and $L$ is its maximizing length. Writing $h=H_2(\\log_3 2)$ and $\\rho=2^{h-1}$, we prove $d_t\\asymp\\rho^t(t+1)^{-3/2}$. In samples through $X$, the same order holds simultaneously up to a fixed distance below $k=\\lfloor\\log_2 X\\rfloor$. A fixed fraction of survivors there meet and strictly descend within a bounded window. The relative error against $Xd_t$ is $O((k+1)^4 2^{-\\kappa(k-t)})$ for an absolute $\\kappa>0$. The limiting mean lies in $[1.562910513896,1.632124257924]$ and is attained by Cesàro means with growing integer caps $B=o(X^{1-h}(\\log X)^{3/2})$. A 26-digit ternary certificate from complete integer orbit traces gives $\\operatorname{dens}\\{x:H(x)=3\\}>4.418\\times10^{-13}$. We also obtain survival bounds on prescribed ascent cylinders and positive-density families of arbitrarily late first meetings with a unique smaller arrival and independent future parity bits. The general lower order of individual clock atoms remains open. The proof of the survival estimate combines bounded-gain bridges with a stopped inverse-word comparison. The finite-sample results use marked returns and a truncation in both reverse length and odd count to control arrivals at arbitrary visitor times.",
